{
 "title": "Measuring inequality and social stratification with Lorenz curvature - Replication Spec",
 "hypothesis": "Family of Lorenz-curvature indices Ip = ||kappa(L)||_p with p in [1,inf], alpha=p-1 stratification-aversion. Axioms hold iff p=1 (alpha=0, weakly Pigou-Dalton). For alpha>0 curvature detects class boundaries; rankings deviate from Gini, Atkinson, Amato-Kakwani, Theil, P90/P10, Wolfson, more for income- than consumption-based countries.",
 "data_sources": [
  "World Bank Poverty and Inequality Platform (PIP) percentile shares https://github.com/PIP-Technical-Team/pip-api - CC-BY 4.0",
  "Processed per-country Lorenz data + code: https://github.com/ahippelainen/curvature_inequality"
 ],
 "sample": "World Bank PIP percentile-share data. Filter: latest yearly dataset available for each nation. Split by welfare concept as reported in PIP: income-based vs consumption-based. Lorenz curve assumed: L:[0,1]->[0,1], L(0)=0, L(1)=1, L in C^a(0,1) a>=2, monotone increasing, convex, no negative values, finite non-zero mean mu. Visualization/alpha-variation and ranking tables use subsamples of 12+12 nations from PIP. Full rank-correlation matrices use all PIP nations with available data.",
 "period_start": "not stated",
 "period_end": "not stated",
 "method": [
  "1. From PIP collect percentile shares per country, keep latest year per country. Split into income-reported and consumption-reported sets.",
  "2. Continuous Lorenz approximation for each country via Bernstein polynomials of order M=20 (robustness M=15,25): L(x)=sum_{k=0}^M c_k B_{k,M}(x), B_{k,M}=binomial(M,k) x^k (1-x)^{M-k}, constraints c0=0, cM=1, c_k <= c_{k+1}, c_{k+2}-2c_{k+1}+c_k >=0, minimize squared residuals. Alternative approximations tested: finite-difference derivative (FDD) and Savitzky-Golay cubic fit to first and last 13 points - rejected due to negative L'(0) in sparse data; Bernstein used for all reported indices.",
  "3. Numerical evaluation: sample fitted L on Gauss-Lobatto grid, derivatives via pseudospectral differentiation matrices to ensure convexity and stable evaluation.",
  "4. Curvature: kappa(L(x)) = L''(x) / (1 + (L'(x))^2)^{3/2} (absolute value removed by convexity).",
  "5. Index family: Ip = ( integral_0^1 kappa(L(x))^p dx )^{1/p} = ||kappa||_p, 1<=p<=inf, p=inf sup norm. Stratification-aversion alpha=p-1. For p=1 closed form: I1 = L'(1)/sqrt(1+L'(1)^2) - L'(0)/sqrt(1+L'(0)^2) where L'(1)=y_max/mu, L'(0)=y_min/mu. Operationalized I1_delta = L'(1-delta)/sqrt(1+L'(1-delta)^2) - L'(delta)/sqrt(1+L'(delta)^2) with delta=0.01 (1st to 99th percentile) for tail-robustness.",
  "6. Axiomatic counterexamples: Lorenz dominance / Pigou-Dalton failure constructed as L1(x)=x^3 vs L2(x)= x +0.5*( sqrt((x-0.5)^2+epsilon) - sqrt(0.25+epsilon) ) with epsilon=1e-3. Compute Ip for varying p and locate crossover where Ip(L1)=Ip(L2).",
  "7. Normalizability divergence sequence: F_epsilon(x)=x-x0+sqrt((x-x0)^2+epsilon), L_epsilon=(F_epsilon-F_epsilon(0))/(F_epsilon(1)-F_epsilon(0)) with x0=3/4 illustrative, show integral diverges as epsilon->0 for p>1.",
  "8. Pigou-Dalton continuous variation: L_epsilon=L+epsilon phi, phi in C_inf_c(a,b), [a,b] subset [0,1], phi(a)=phi'(a)=phi''(a)=0, phi(b)=phi'(b)=phi''(b)=0, phi>=0, derive sufficient condition (kappa^{p-1}/A^3)'' + (3 L'' L' kappa^{p-1}/A^5)' <0 with A=sqrt(1+(L')^2).",
  "9. Alpha-variation visualization: compute Ip for alpha in [0.01,10] (p in [1.01,11]) on 12 income +12 consumption visualization subsamples. Rankings stable outside window. Note qualitatively affected by sparsity for alpha !=0.",
  "10. Comparison indices in Lorenz form (continuous) with fixed parameters: G=1-2 int_0^1 L dx; A_0.5=1-(int_0^1 sqrt(L')dx)^2 with Atkinson epsilon=0.5; K=(int_0^1 sqrt(1+(L')^2)dx - sqrt(2))/(2-sqrt(2)); T1=int_0^1 L' ln(L')dx; P90/P10=L'(0.9)/L'(0.1); W=2*(1-2L(0.5)-G)/L'(0.5). Compute country rankings 1=most equal.",
  "11. Rank correlations: for full 72 income and 100 consumption sets compute orderings under 7 indices (I1,G,A0.5,K,T1,P90/P10,W) and Spearman and Kendall correlation matrices (figures only, no numeric values printed). Robustness of Kendall differences tested with order-15,20,25 Bernstein fits."
 ],
